## Third-party data (retain their original licenses)

The following bundled files are derived from or copied from third-party
resources and are governed by their original terms, NOT by CC-BY-4.0:

- `scatac/data/20260216202920_JASPAR2026_combined_matrices_1336868.meme` and
  `scatac/data/motifs_annotations_full.txt`: transcription-factor binding motifs
  from the JASPAR database (https://jaspar.elixir.no/), released under CC0.
  Please cite JASPAR if you reuse these motifs.

- scATAC-seq sample metadata and atlas-derived tables (under `scatac/data/`)
  are derived from the publicly available human single-cell
  chromatin-accessibility atlas (601,093 cells, 108 cell types, 28 tissues) of
  Zhang et al., 2021:

    K. Zhang, J. D. Hocker, M. Miller, X. Hou, J. Chiou, O. B. Poirion,
    Y. Qiu, Y. E. Li, K. J. Gaulton, A. Wang, S. Preissl, and B. Ren.
    "A single-cell atlas of chromatin accessibility in the human genome."
    Cell, 184(24):5985-6001.e19, Nov. 2021. doi:10.1016/j.cell.2021.10.024

  Source data: http://catlas.org/. Please cite Zhang et al. (2021) if you
  reuse these atlas-derived tables.

- cfDNA fragmentation data underlying the cfDNA tables are from the cohort of
  Cristiano et al., 2019 (Nature 570:385-389) and are publicly available through
  FinaleDB (Zheng et al., 2021, Bioinformatics 37:2502-2503,
  doi:10.1093/bioinformatics/btaa999; http://finaledb.research.cchmc.org/), a
  database of cell-free DNA
  fragmentation patterns. Please cite Cristiano et al. (2019) and
  FinaleDB if you reuse these.

## Human-subject (clinical) data

The cfDNA and kidney prediction tables contain model outputs for de-identified
clinical samples (pseudonymous sample codes only; no names, dates, or other
identifiers). The independently profiled kidney cohort is in-house. Public
release is covered by the studies' informed consent and institutional approvals.
